      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The 1940s were defined by an irreconcilable clash between the British desire to maintain control and the rising, mutually exclusive demands of the Indian National Congress for a united India versus the Muslim League's insistence on a separate homeland. This fundamental disagreement over sovereignty and religious identity was exacerbated by the British refusal to implement a power-sharing formula, which ultimately forced the hand of the League and the Congress into a violent struggle for independence. The partition was not a peaceful administrative decision but a tragic, blood-soaked solution born from decades of communal polarization and the failure of political compromise.",
      "source": "target",
